{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>But as for you — do not….</b> Meaning: all the categories of fear exist with respect to others, but not with you. And he was precise in using the vav of ״וְאַתֶּם״ to intimate: even after they justified the words of the spies and spoke improper things." "<b>For they are our bread.</b> The reason they expressed the matter in the language of bread will be explained according to the words of men of truth who investigated the act of HaShem in forming living creatures such that every living thing eats and drinks — for HaShem could have made it so that living creatures would be sustained without food. And behold, before you is a species of creature that inhales air and that serves as its nourishment. And if HaShem had done thus, Israel would be free to engage in Torah and the commandments; also they would have been prevented from many improper deeds that are brought about by the needs of sustenance and livelihood." "And on this basis I would offer an explanation on my own: that HaShem wished to merit Israel with the 613 commandments, the majority of which depend on species of food and drink — terumot, tithes, hallah, the commandment of shemittah, and those involving sowing, and plowing, and reaping, and the like." "But the sages of truth (Sha'ar HaGilgulim, ch. 4) added in this a general reason for praise: for all the essences of created beings ascend in holiness through the sod of the refinement of the sparks of holiness — these are those that are refined by means of foods. From this point on, sparks of holiness are called ״the bread of the people of HaShem.״ They also said, of blessed memory, that every created being contains a holy spark, even in impure creatures, and in wicked people, and even in Samael the wicked — there is none among them that does not contain a vital portion, which is the aspect of good, which is the thing that sustains it, without which its counterpart could not endure at all; and when the vital spark is separated from its place, the entire structure is nullified and lost. They also said (ibid., ch. 18) that the aspect of holiness, by its nature, draws from itself like a magnet stone, and with this you will understand their saying, of blessed memory (Shabbat 34a), that ״he fixed his gaze upon it and it became a heap of bones״ — and understand. And according to these words, the intent of their saying ״Do not fear… for they are <b>our bread</b>״ is directed: the species of bread that we speak of is ״their shadow has departed from over them״ — the spark that sustains them has already been removed from them. And further, <b>and HaShem is with us</b> — which is the aspect of the totality of holiness, and thereby it becomes like a magnet stone for all the sparks that sustain them, and they all perish together. From this point on there is no room to fear them, as they said: ״Do not fear them.״"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:08:55.727769442Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}